    <item>
  <title>EU Kids Act - Wie soziale Medien für Kinder sicherer werden sollen</title>
  <link>https://www.deutschlandfunk.de/eu-kids-act-social-media-kinder-jugendschutz-100.html</link>
  <description>
    <![CDATA[<img src="https://bilder.deutschlandfunk.de/03/ef/7f/27/03ef7f27-1a60-4316-abf2-61302bee9cfe/eu-kids-act-social-media-tiktok-100-1920x1920.jpg" alt="The TikTok logo is displayed on a smartphone screen in this photo illustration in Brussels, Belgium, on March 30, 2026. The platform faces regulatory scrutiny in Europe regarding concerns about design features and potential impacts on children&apos;s mental health under the EU&apos;s Digital Services Act. (Photo by Jonathan Raa/NurPhoto)" title="The TikTok logo is displayed on a smartphone screen in this photo illustration in Brussels, Belgium, on March 30, 2026. The platform faces regulatory scrutiny in Europe regarding concerns about design features and potential impacts on children&apos;s mental health under the EU&apos;s Digital Services Act. (Photo by Jonathan Raa/NurPhoto)" width="1920" height="1920" border="0" align="left" hspace="4" vspace="4"/>Mit ihrem Kids Act fordert die EU bei sozialen Medien ein Verbot von Suchtfaktoren, KI-Tricks und Fremdkontakten. Ein Stufenplan soll den Zugang ab 13 Jahren regeln. Wie realistisch ist das und wie könnten sichere Social Media für Kinder aussehen?<br clear="all"/><br/><p><br/></p>]]>
  </description>
